WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.535
-You know our next guest
from her work

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:04.870
in "The Big Bang Theory,"
"True Blood," and "The Bronze."

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.207
She stars in "Night Court,"
which premiers January 17th

00:00:07.207 --> 00:00:09.375
right here on NBC.
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:11.444
-I almost didn't recognize you
with a beard.

00:00:11.444 --> 00:00:14.214
In pictures, you always look
so put together.

00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:16.249
Not that the beard
isn't a good look.

00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:19.185
It's a great way to cover up
a weird neck.

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:20.620
Not that your neck is weird.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:24.623
It's --
Wow! Look at that view.

00:00:24.623 --> 00:00:27.159
-That is what sold Sarah
on the place.

00:00:27.159 --> 00:00:29.095
-Sarah your wife?

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:31.831
-She was.

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:34.333
So, what brings you to town?

00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:36.735
-I moved here for a job.

00:00:36.735 --> 00:00:38.504
I am the new night-court judge.

00:00:38.504 --> 00:00:41.707
[ Scream ]

00:00:41.707 --> 00:00:44.477
-Please welcome to the show,
Melissa Rauch, everybody!

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:46.646
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:52.018 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:57.356 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:57.356 --> 00:00:59.525
Welcome to the show, Melissa!

00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:02.394
Oh, my God, thank you
for wearing sweatpants.

00:01:02.394 --> 00:01:05.431
-Well, thank you,
for not kicking me out of here.

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:07.132
Look, here's the deal.
-Yeah.

00:01:07.132 --> 00:01:08.401
-It's January 5th.
-Yeah.

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:10.069
-I've been in sweatpants
for three weeks.

00:01:10.069 --> 00:01:12.571
To be honest, I've been in
sweatpants for three years.

00:01:12.571 --> 00:01:15.474
And I tried.
I got into hair and makeup.

00:01:15.474 --> 00:01:17.777
I tried to take the next step,
and I couldn't do it.

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:19.379
-All right.
-So I apologi--

00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:22.115
I need maximum waistband
flexibility at all times.

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:23.716
-We're so happy!
-That's what's going on.

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:25.484
-I'm so happy
about your honesty.

00:01:25.484 --> 00:01:27.152
I will say, I am very --
you know, obviously,

00:01:27.152 --> 00:01:29.555
post-pandemic, I have kept it
a little more casual,

00:01:29.555 --> 00:01:32.024
and I'm very happy with it.
But sometimes I feel --

00:01:32.024 --> 00:01:34.360
When I feel the worst about it
is when somebody comes up

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.262
very dressed up.
-Oh, well I'm so glad

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:38.197
I can make you feel --
You know what? Here's the deal.

00:01:38.197 --> 00:01:40.599
I --
The people that love me the most

00:01:40.599 --> 00:01:41.968
and the people
that I love the most

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:46.206
know that I will look like
a garbage person in front of you

00:01:46.206 --> 00:01:48.007
if I love you.
-Ahh.

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:51.411
-So, I'm a big fan, and this
is actually a sign of love

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:54.547
that I look like trash.
-Oh, my God.

00:01:54.547 --> 00:01:57.484
I accept this sign of love.
-Thank you, thank you.

00:01:57.484 --> 00:02:00.819
-We -- Speaking of things we
love, we both got young kids.

00:02:00.819 --> 00:02:02.388
You've got a 5 and a 2.
-I do.

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:04.523
How is that going?
-It's awesome.

00:02:04.523 --> 00:02:05.891
It's awesome.
They're the best.

00:02:05.891 --> 00:02:08.228
They're very --
they're into this stage now

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:10.529
where they're just
climbing on everything.

00:02:10.529 --> 00:02:12.331
So I'm constantly trying to see

00:02:12.331 --> 00:02:13.766
what they're
gonna jump off of.

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:17.103
And I'm always telling them,
"We can't climb on things."

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:20.706
But I haven't reached 5'0" yet.
-Yeah.

00:02:20.706 --> 00:02:22.408
-I'm holding out hope.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:22.408 --> 00:02:24.677
So I climb on things --
-You're a climber.

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:27.346
-I'm a climber.
I have to get dishes down.

00:02:27.346 --> 00:02:30.150
-So who took this photo?

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:31.584
-Yeah, my kid
took that photo of me.

00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:32.685
-So this is, like, very --

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:34.119
So your kid
basically caught you.

00:02:34.119 --> 00:02:36.822
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:02:36.822 --> 00:02:38.991
Yeah, that --
-So that's hard for you to say,

00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:40.226
like, "No climbing."

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:41.761
-I'm so not practicing
what I'm preaching.

00:02:41.761 --> 00:02:44.864
I also want to point out,
this is my normal attire.

00:02:44.864 --> 00:02:47.767
[ Laughter ]
So this is actually dressed up.

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:50.069
-Yeah.
-Like, this is an ensemble

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:51.371
compared to what
I'm wearing there.

00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:52.739
-Now we've established
that this is fancy.

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:54.974
-Exactly, yeah.
-This is very cool.

00:02:54.974 --> 00:02:57.410
As we saw from the clip,
that's John Larroquette,

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:59.912
who is one of the stars
of the original "Night Court."

00:02:59.912 --> 00:03:02.048
How has it been so far
to work with someone like this,

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:04.016
a television legend?
-Oh, my gosh, he is --

00:03:04.016 --> 00:03:06.051
He's the greatest.
He's the best of the best.

00:03:06.051 --> 00:03:08.587
I grew up such a fan of his,
and so to work with him

00:03:08.587 --> 00:03:10.656
is just such a dream come true.

00:03:10.656 --> 00:03:14.493
We had this fun moment
where it was like halfway --

00:03:14.493 --> 00:03:16.161
No, it was towards the end
of the season,

00:03:16.161 --> 00:03:19.365
and we had this scene where
we're yelling at each other,

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:21.567
and, of course,
we've established I'm,

00:03:21.567 --> 00:03:23.402
you know, not the tallest.

00:03:23.402 --> 00:03:25.738
And he's about 6'4."
And it's like,

00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:29.008
"Well, how can we make this
just a little more evened out?

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:31.678
So we are so lucky
to have the iconic

00:03:31.678 --> 00:03:34.613
original "Night Court" couch.
And I was like, "Well, I'll just

00:03:34.613 --> 00:03:38.384
jump up on this so that maybe
we're a little bit more,

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:43.622
you know, eye to eye,
and I jump up, and I go flying.

00:03:43.622 --> 00:03:46.258
Like,
just up in the air so high.

00:03:46.258 --> 00:03:48.594
People said I was like
perpendicular to the ground.

00:03:48.594 --> 00:03:50.696
And it was one of those moments
where I was like,

00:03:50.696 --> 00:03:53.265
"I should have
hit the ground already."

00:03:53.265 --> 00:03:55.167
Like, "I have been up here.
I'm not that tall.

00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:57.436
Like, I should have --
My legs should have come down,"

00:03:57.436 --> 00:03:59.805
and I just felt like
I kept on going and going.

00:03:59.805 --> 00:04:03.809
And I look up and I see John,
who's, like, trying to help me.

00:04:03.809 --> 00:04:06.612
And, you know, he's got a very
distinguished, handsome look.

00:04:06.612 --> 00:04:08.381
He sort of looks now
like one of those

00:04:08.381 --> 00:04:10.416
Renaissance depictions of God.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:12.785
And I'm looking at him, like,
"Is this it?

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:16.155
Is this lights out for me?"
And John Larroquette, as God,

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:18.757
was welcoming me in,
and that's how I go?

00:04:18.757 --> 00:04:20.526
I was kind of okay with it.
It was good.

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:21.894
-I'm glad you survived.

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:23.630
I'm glad you lived
to tell a tale about it.

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:24.663
-Yeah.

00:04:24.663 --> 00:04:26.265
-You got your start
as a comedian.

00:04:26.265 --> 00:04:28.567
You did stand-up
here in the city.

00:04:28.567 --> 00:04:29.869
You're an East Coaster.

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:33.005
How were your early stand-up
days here in the city?

00:04:33.005 --> 00:04:35.541
-They were great.
It was sort of tough stuff.

00:04:35.541 --> 00:04:37.242
You sort of
had to earn your stripes.

00:04:37.242 --> 00:04:40.479
And I was one of those people
who would hand out fliers

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:43.182
in Times Square, like, "Come see
comedy, come see comedy."

00:04:43.182 --> 00:04:45.818
And then people would just
chuck a flier at your head.

00:04:45.818 --> 00:04:51.257
And one of the moments
that sort of was a bit of a push

00:04:51.257 --> 00:04:53.860
to move to L.A. was,
I was standing outside

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:55.694
a Broadway show,
and I was trying to get people

00:04:55.694 --> 00:04:57.763
to come to the late show, and it
was the middle of February.

00:04:57.763 --> 00:05:00.833
And I was like, "Come see
comedy, come see comedy."

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:03.569
And this little girl just looked
at me, and looked at her mother

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:05.905
and was like,
"Mommy, that is so sad."

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:08.808
[ Laughter ]
I was like, "Yeah."

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:09.976
Right.

00:05:09.976 --> 00:05:13.846
-You had a very novel
approach to dating.

00:05:13.846 --> 00:05:15.414
I guess must have been
when you were here.

00:05:15.414 --> 00:05:17.716
Explain what
garbage-pile jumping was.

00:05:17.716 --> 00:05:20.252
-Okay.
So, this --

00:05:20.252 --> 00:05:22.955
I look back on my early days
in New York, on my 20s,

00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:24.823
and I was a much more
carefree person.

00:05:24.823 --> 00:05:27.627
Like, now I am like
hand sanitizer is --

00:05:27.627 --> 00:05:29.795
I basically bathe in it
every night.

00:05:29.795 --> 00:05:31.864
But back in the day, you know,

00:05:31.864 --> 00:05:33.933
New York, the streets
are lined with trash.

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:35.034
-Yep.
-A lot of the times.

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:36.435
-Not just back in the day.

00:05:36.435 --> 00:05:37.636
[ Laughter ]
-True.

00:05:37.636 --> 00:05:39.505
-We're having a real
throwback era right now.

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:40.773
[ Laughter ]
-Okay, it's good to know --

00:05:40.773 --> 00:05:42.708
-We're very retro
with our garbage, yeah.

00:05:42.708 --> 00:05:44.843
-It's good to know
that I could still do it.

00:05:44.843 --> 00:05:47.647
So, I would walk down the street
with whoever I was dating

00:05:47.647 --> 00:05:50.649
at the time,
and I would use this

00:05:50.649 --> 00:05:54.320
as a barometer to see if they
were worthy of a second date.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:56.355
I would just be --
we would be mid-conversation,

00:05:56.355 --> 00:06:01.060
and I would just hurl myself
into a pile of trash.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.128
And if they laughed,
I'd be like, "Yeah, all right.

00:06:03.128 --> 00:06:05.798
I can roll with you."
It's insane.

00:06:05.798 --> 00:06:08.233
There could have been
dead bodies,

00:06:08.233 --> 00:06:10.436
hypodermic needles in there,
rats.

00:06:10.436 --> 00:06:12.271
-No, we don't have
the best of the trash.

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:14.907
-No!
That is not high-class trash.

00:06:14.907 --> 00:06:17.009
But I was like, "Okay, if --"

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:19.612
Not,
"Oh, come and meet my parents,

00:06:19.612 --> 00:06:20.946
Do you want to have children?"

00:06:20.946 --> 00:06:24.116
No, if I -- if you laugh
when I try and kill myself

00:06:24.116 --> 00:06:26.552
in some sewage,
then you can be my husband."

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:28.654
-Well, now, it seems like
this maybe did bear out

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:30.589
to be the case because
you are writing partners

00:06:30.589 --> 00:06:31.924
with your husband,
so he's obviously

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:34.660
a very funny person, as well.
And as collab--

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:37.363
When you write together,
how does that work?

00:06:37.363 --> 00:06:40.832
How is the spousal dynamic when
you sit down to try to be funny?

00:06:40.832 --> 00:06:43.369
-Well, here's the deal.
-Okay.

00:06:43.369 --> 00:06:46.672
-Like, I --
We're on a set together.

00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:47.707
And, you know, we're both --

00:06:47.707 --> 00:06:48.908
-He writes
for "Night Court" now.

00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:50.509
-He's a producer on it.
-Okay, great.

00:06:50.509 --> 00:06:54.112
-And when you work together
and you live together,

00:06:54.112 --> 00:06:57.282
you can't -- there's no
water-cooler chat, right?

00:06:57.282 --> 00:06:59.885
Like, I can't be like,
"What'd you do last night?"

00:06:59.885 --> 00:07:03.088
I know we argued over
who's gonna do the dishes,

00:07:03.088 --> 00:07:05.391
and then we like watched
10 minutes of a Hallmark movie

00:07:05.391 --> 00:07:06.458
and fell asleep.
-Right.

00:07:06.458 --> 00:07:09.094
-I know.
Or if he was late,

00:07:09.094 --> 00:07:11.430
he couldn't say
that he was stuck in traffic,

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:13.399
because I saw him
sitting at the kitchen table

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:14.967
playing fantasy football.
-Right, yeah.

00:07:14.967 --> 00:07:16.702
-Like, it's not gonna happen.

00:07:16.702 --> 00:07:19.372
And then, also,
when you're married to someone

00:07:19.372 --> 00:07:24.877
for a long time, you -- you
build up resentments at home.

00:07:24.877 --> 00:07:27.913
And then, what's great about
working together is then you can

00:07:27.913 --> 00:07:30.082
build up all the resentments
professionally, too.

00:07:30.082 --> 00:07:31.183
[ Laughter ]

00:07:31.183 --> 00:07:32.284
It sounds like
we're on the rocks.

00:07:32.284 --> 00:07:34.720
We're not.
-You're better than that.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:35.921
-We love each other.
We just want

00:07:35.921 --> 00:07:37.823
all the opportunities
to resent each other.

00:07:37.823 --> 00:07:40.559
-That's lovely.
You grew up in New Jersey.

00:07:40.559 --> 00:07:43.662
You do not have, I would say,
a New Jersey accent.

00:07:43.662 --> 00:07:44.764
Was that always been the case?

00:07:44.764 --> 00:07:46.832
-I'm so glad it hasn't come out.

00:07:46.832 --> 00:07:49.301
Yeah, it was a really,
really thick accent

00:07:49.301 --> 00:07:51.003
when I first went
to acting school.

00:07:51.003 --> 00:07:53.205
They were mortified.
I just came in,

00:07:53.205 --> 00:07:54.440
I'm like, [New Jersey accent]
"Hi! How's everyone doin'?

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:55.975
What are you talkin' --
Romeo, Romeo,

00:07:55.975 --> 00:07:57.876
wherefore art thou Romeo?"
[ Laughter ]

00:07:57.876 --> 00:08:00.145
[ Normal voice ] Yeah.
The teachers were like,

00:08:00.145 --> 00:08:03.049
"Go to a voice and speech class
immediately."

00:08:03.049 --> 00:08:04.816
And they did.
It took a while to lose it.

00:08:04.816 --> 00:08:06.952
It comes out.
-And I know you got to see

00:08:06.952 --> 00:08:09.021
some family obviously
on this trip, which is great.

00:08:09.021 --> 00:08:11.757
You did the Macy's
Thanksgiving Day Parade,

00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:14.059
which was a cool way to promote
"Night Court."

00:08:14.059 --> 00:08:16.962
Was your family excited to see
you doing something like that?

00:08:16.962 --> 00:08:19.398
-You would think they would be.
-Yeah.

00:08:19.398 --> 00:08:22.268
-I -- I honestly --
I thought it was like

00:08:22.268 --> 00:08:24.070
the crown jewel
of my professional career,

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:26.638
getting to -- it was just,
like, a recorded segment,

00:08:26.638 --> 00:08:29.207
saying -- they asked me
a few questions about like,

00:08:29.207 --> 00:08:31.010
"What's your
Thanksgiving family traditions?"

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:33.779
And I was like, "My family,
who I've watched this with

00:08:33.779 --> 00:08:36.515
every year since I'm a kid,
they're gonna lose their minds."

00:08:36.515 --> 00:08:40.252
And so the clip that got on,
I said something like,

00:08:40.252 --> 00:08:43.723
"One of my favorite Thanksgiving
traditions is arguing

00:08:43.723 --> 00:08:46.592
with my family, and we're from
Jersey, and we do it well."

00:08:46.592 --> 00:08:50.028
And my mom, like,
didn't mention anything

00:08:50.028 --> 00:08:51.397
at Thanksgiving about it.

00:08:51.397 --> 00:08:54.099
And I was like, "Did you guys
see the parade?"

00:08:54.099 --> 00:08:55.901
Mom's like "Mm-hmm."
[ Laughter ]

00:08:55.901 --> 00:08:58.804
"Yeah.
We saw it, yeah.

00:08:58.804 --> 00:09:01.273
A lot of my friends called,
and they saw it, too,

00:09:01.273 --> 00:09:03.942
and said they saw you."
I was like, "Did you like it?"

00:09:03.942 --> 00:09:06.745
She goes, "Yeah, you were great,
you were great.

00:09:06.745 --> 00:09:08.980
You said we argued.
We don't argue so much.

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:10.782
I mean, we argue."
[ Laughter ]

00:09:10.782 --> 00:09:11.917
"We totally argue!
What are you talking about?

00:09:11.917 --> 00:09:13.586
All we do is argue!"
"We don't argue!"

00:09:13.586 --> 00:09:15.321
[ Laughter ]

00:09:15.321 --> 00:09:16.522
-Well, that is a perfect --

00:09:16.522 --> 00:09:18.490
I would say that's a new
Thanksgiving tradition...

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:19.725
-Exactly.
-...for you to start an argument

00:09:19.725 --> 00:09:21.460
during the parade
that can carry over.

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:22.594
-Exactly, agree.

00:09:22.594 --> 00:09:23.929
-Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:09:23.929 --> 00:09:25.564
Congratulations on the show.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:25.564 --> 00:09:27.399
Melissa Rauch, everybody!

00:09:27.399 --> 00:09:29.968
"Night Court" premieres
January 17th here on NBC.

00:09:29.968 --> 00:09:31.537
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

